> **Part 8: The Promise and the Mirror** *(you are here)*

*This is My Ledger, the personal side of v64otd.com. This series is history first; this final part is mostly opinion, marked as such, built on the 2026 results.*

*Disclosure: I endorsed independent write-in candidate Jenn Mack for Texas governor and voted Libertarian in most of my other races.*

> **Mandate:** the authority a winner claims voters gave them to carry out their platform. Winners always claim one. Voters rarely grant one.

#### The Promise

Here’s the sentence this whole series has been building toward:

**Government is a promise made on behalf of everyone and enforced against anyone. That’s why it has to serve everyone.**

Every law is passed in the name of all of us, and every law is enforced against any one of us, whether we voted for the people who wrote it or not. A tax, a regulation, a criminal statute, an emergency order: none of them asks how you voted before it applies to you.

That’s why faction is so dangerous, and why Madison spent [*Federalist No. 10*](https://constitutioncenter.org/the-constitution/historic-document-library/detail/james-madison-federalist-10-1788) on it. When one faction takes the government, it holds a promise made to everyone and the power to enforce it against anyone. The temptation is to use that power for its own side and against the other.

#### The Wave

On November 3, 2026, voters handed Democrats control of the U.S. House and Senate. In Texas, the wave went all the way down the ballot. Democrat Gina Hinojosa was elected governor, the first Democrat to win the office since Ann Richards. Democrat Vikki Goodwin beat incumbent Dan Patrick for lieutenant governor, the first Democrat elected to that post since Bob Bullock. Democrat James Talarico won the U.S. Senate seat. Democrats also won every other statewide office on the ballot: attorney general, comptroller, land commissioner, agriculture commissioner and railroad commissioner, along with all four Texas Supreme Court seats that were up. In the Legislature, the Associated Press projected that Democrats flipped the Texas House, with at least 76 of its 150 seats, while Republicans kept the Texas Senate, 19 seats to 12. These are unofficial returns; the state canvass comes later this month.

As Part 3 showed, voters said plainly why. About three-quarters rated the economy poorly. About 8 in 10 said inflation had caused hardship for their households. 62% said starting the war with Iran was the wrong decision. And just under half viewed the Democratic Party favorably, not much better than Republicans. It was an eviction, not an endorsement.

#### The Mirror for the Winners

The winners didn’t wait long to read it differently. Senate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer said, “We listened to the American people... We listened. They didn’t.” House Democratic leader Hakeem Jeffries said, “The American people are crying out for transformational change.”

**How the press told it.** In Part 7, I promised to compare the press coverage of this wave with that of the Republican waves of 2010 and 2014. Here’s what the morning-after front pages said:

- **2010:** “In a broad rebuke to President Obama’s agenda, Republicans have taken back the House of Representatives,” the *Christian Science Monitor* reported. President Obama himself called it a “shellacking.”
- **2014:** The *New York Daily News* ran one word over the Democrats’ defeat: “NOPE.” The *New York Post* went with “STRIPPED! Emperor has no clothes.” The *Kansas City Star* called it “Red Tuesday.”
- **2026:** The *New York Post* ran “Blue Tsunami.” The *Chicago Sun-Times* went with “Dem-olition.” The *Dallas Morning News* called it a “Wave of change.”

A handful of headlines can’t prove or disprove bias, and these don’t. The tabloids mocked the losers in every wave, whichever side lost, and the same *New York Post* that mocked Obama in 2014 played this year’s wave straight. The pattern that holds is the one from Part 7: every wave is covered as a scoreboard between two teams. Not one of those headlines, in any of the three years, was about anyone outside the two parties.

We’ve seen this before, from both sides. Democrats won Congress in 2006 and the White House in 2008, treating it as a mandate; voters handed the House to Republicans in 2010. Republicans won the White House and Congress in 2016 and treated that as a mandate; voters handed the House to Democrats in 2018. Every wave has been followed by overreach, and every overreach has been followed by a wave going the other way. Washington called it “the alternate domination of one faction over another,” and warned that it “is itself a frightful despotism.”

Texas is the sharpest test. For more than 30 years, Texas Democrats complained about Republican mid-decade redistricting, about Republican governors using emergency powers, about an attorney general using state investigations against political opponents, and about laws written to punish the other side’s allies. Now Democrats hold the governor’s office, the attorney general’s office, the lieutenant governor’s office, which runs the Texas Senate even though Republicans kept a majority of its seats, and, by the AP’s projection, the Texas House.

> *Editorial:* Here’s the mirror. If the new majority uses that power the way it complained the old one did, redrawing maps for its own advantage, targeting the other side’s donors and organizations, governing by emergency, it will prove it was never against the abuse. It was only against losing. The test isn’t what they do for the voters who elected them. It’s whether they keep the promise for the millions who didn’t.

#### The Mirror for the Losers

Republicans have a mirror too. Dan Patrick, who led the Texas Senate for over a decade, conceded the morning after: “It was a fair election, and I accept the majority will of the voters.” That’s the standard, and it deserves credit. Not every losing candidate in recent years, in either party, has met it.

The next test for the losers is whether they become a real opposition or a sore one. A real opposition holds the majority to the promise: it demands transparency, fights overreach, and offers better ideas. A sore opposition spends two years trying to make sure nothing works, so it can run on the failure. That’s the parasite-and-host cycle from Part 1, and both parties have played both roles.

#### The Mirror for Us

Now the hardest mirror. This one is for those of us who want something other than the two parties, and that includes me.

A record 45% of Americans call themselves independents. Most said they were angry this fall about inflation, the cost of living, and the war. And yet the anger went almost entirely to the other major party.

- **Libertarians in Texas** drew between about 1.5% and 2.6% in the statewide races, in unofficial returns. Pat Dixon got about 1.5% for governor; Anthony Cristo, the party’s best showing, got about 2.6% for lieutenant governor.
- **The independent slate lost everywhere.** Seven independents ran this fall as a slate backed by Independents Win PAC, with the goal of building a “Fulcrum Caucus” that neither party could control. In Nebraska, Dan Osborn lost to Republican Sen. Pete Ricketts, 50% to 44%. In Montana, Seth Bodnar got about 38%. In Idaho, Todd Achilles got about 34%. In South Dakota, Brian Bengs got about 40%. In California, Rep. Kevin Kiley, who left the Republican Party and refused to caucus with either side, lost his redrawn district to Democrat Richard Pan, 57.5% to 42.5%. In Alaska’s House race, independent Bill Hill trailed Republican Rep. Nick Begich on first choices, with the ranked-choice count set for November 18.
- **Write-in votes,** including Jenn Mack’s for governor, aren’t counted in the unofficial returns. They’ll be reported when the counties finish their canvass.

There’s some good news. Under Texas law, a minor party keeps its automatic place on the ballot as long as one of its statewide candidates has reached 2% of the vote in at least one of the past five general elections. The Libertarians cleared that bar in 2024, and in unofficial returns they cleared it again this year in the races for lieutenant governor, comptroller and agriculture commissioner. They’ll stay on the ballot. And in Alaska, voters appear to be keeping open primaries and ranked-choice voting for a second time: with about three-quarters of precincts reporting, the repeal was losing, 52% to 48%. The race hasn’t been called. If the result holds, it’s a small but real sign that the locks from Part 6 can be opened.

> *Editorial:* The uncomfortable truth is that the Uniparty didn’t lose on November 3. It traded places. The machine from Part 3, the locks from Part 6, the megaphone from Part 7, and the fear from Part 1 all worked exactly as designed: angry voters had exactly one place to go, and they went there. Those of us who believe in third parties and independents haven’t yet given most voters a reason to believe the alternative is real.

#### What Citizens Can Do Between Elections

The parties count on us forgetting until the next election. Here’s how not to:

1. **Hold the winners to the promise.** Write to your new representatives and ask: What will you do for the people in your district who voted against you?
1. **Watch the maps.** If the new majority in any state starts redrawing districts for its own advantage, call it out, no matter which party does it.
1. **Watch the emergency powers.** The power the last majority used against its opponents is now in someone else’s hands. Ask whether it should exist at all.
1. **Push for the keys from Parts 6 and 7:** fair ballot access, open primaries, ranked-choice voting, repeal of sore loser laws, independent redistricting, real-time donor disclosure on every political ad, AI labels and “Stand by Your Ad” for digital ads, electoral votes split by district, guaranteed floor votes for bills with majority support, and seats for independents on debate commissions and congressional committees. In Texas, start with the right to put reforms on the ballot yourselves, and with limits on how much a single donor can give to a candidate for state office.
1. **Keep score.** Jeffries has outlined a 100-day agenda focused on lowering the cost of living. Write down what the new majorities promise, in Washington and in Austin, and check it in April. Promises made on behalf of everyone should be checked by everyone.
1. **Vote for something.** Look at every name on your ballot, every time, including the ones without an R or a D.

#### The End of the Series, and the Beginning

The Founders warned us. Madison said in [*Federalist No. 10*](https://constitutioncenter.org/the-constitution/historic-document-library/detail/james-madison-federalist-10-1788) that faction is “sown in the nature of man” and can’t be removed without destroying liberty, only controlled. Washington warned of “the alternate domination of one faction over another.” Adams called a two-party republic “the greatest political evil under our Constitution.”

We built it anyway. The machine bought votes. The Progressives wrote the parties into law. The parties decided who could vote, then whom we could vote for, then spent billions telling us whom to fear. And on November 3, 2026, millions of angry Americans did exactly what the system was built to make them do.

**What liberty means.** The word has come up in every part of this series, so let me say plainly what I mean by it. The Declaration of Independence says every person has the right to “Life, Liberty and the pursuit of Happiness,” and that “to secure these rights, Governments are instituted among Men, deriving their just powers from the consent of the governed.” Government doesn’t give us our rights. It exists to protect them.

Thomas Jefferson gave the best short definition I know. In an 1819 letter, he wrote that “rightful liberty” is “unobstructed action according to our will,” with one limit: “the equal rights of others.” You’re free to live as you choose, right up to the point where your choices take away someone else’s life, liberty, or pursuit of happiness. That’s where government comes in, and that’s where it should stop. Jefferson added a warning: he refused to define liberty as whatever the law allows, “because law is often but the tyrant’s will.”

That’s the guardrail. A government that governs with liberty in mind does two jobs at once. It protects each of us from anyone who would trample our rights, and it restrains itself from becoming the one doing the trampling. Fail at either job, and the promise breaks. A government too weak to protect your rights leaves you at the mercy of whoever is strongest. A government too strong, run by whichever faction won last, becomes the tyrant’s will Jefferson warned about.

> *Editorial:* That’s the test I’d apply to every bill, every party and every candidate, including the ones I vote for. Not “Is it my side?” but “Does it protect one person’s rights without trampling another’s?” A faction can’t pass that test, because a faction exists to win for its side. Citizens can.

Government is a promise made on behalf of everyone and enforced against anyone. It’s a promise to protect every person’s life, liberty, and pursuit of happiness, not to hand the winners power over the losers. The promise belongs to the citizens, not the factions holding it this year. It’s time we started acting like it.

#### Discussion Questions for the Comments

- Was your vote on November 3 for something, or against something?
- What’s one thing the new majority could do that would prove it serves the people who voted against it?
- Did the coverage of this year’s wave sound different to you than 2010 or 2014? Which outlet do you trust to tell you about all your choices?
- What would it take for you to vote for a candidate without an R or a D next to their name?
- Jefferson said your liberty ends where someone else’s equal rights begin. Name one law, from either party, that fails that test.

